course: biology
lesson: 170
title: Antarctica: emperor huddles, krill, clear-blooded fish and stone-stealing penguins
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Antarctica is a frozen continent around the South Pole, colder and windier than anywhere else on Earth, with almost no land animals, while the sea around it is crowded with life.
explain: Almost everything there depends on krill, small shrimp-like animals that swarm in such numbers that they can colour the sea pink, and penguins, seals, fish and the biggest whales all eat them.
explain: In a blizzard thousands of emperor penguins huddle together and slowly shuffle, so the ones on the cold edge take turns in the warm middle, like children crowding together at a cold bus stop.
explain: Cartoons and adverts often show polar bears chasing penguins, but that can never happen in the wild: polar bears live in the Arctic in the far north, and penguins live in the south, on opposite ends of the planet.
fact: Emperor penguins huddle in big groups to keep warm in the Antarctic winter, and the males hold the egg on their feet under a warm fold of skin for about two months without eating while the females feed at sea; partners and chicks find each other in the crowd by their calls.
fact: Antarctic krill are small shrimp-like animals that live in huge swarms, and the weight of all Antarctic krill may be more than the weight of all humans.
fact: Leopard seals are big hunters with powerful jaws and long teeth that catch penguins, and they also eat krill by sieving water through their teeth.
fact: Weddell seals live farther south than any other mammal and keep breathing holes open in the ice, and their underwater calls sound like science fiction lasers.
fact: Antarctic icefish have no red blood cells, so their blood is clear, and many Antarctic fish have antifreeze in their blood that stops ice crystals from growing; icy water holds a lot of oxygen, so clear blood can still carry enough.
fact: Penguins slide on their bellies, called tobogganing, to travel fast on snow, and Adélie penguins can shoot out of the water and jump onto the ice.
fact: Adélie penguins build nests of small stones to keep their eggs above melting snow, and they often steal stones from their neighbours' nests.
fact: Some male penguins give stones to females as part of choosing a partner.
fact: Polar bears live only in the Arctic in the north and penguins live in the southern half of the world, so they never meet in the wild.
fact: The Antarctic midge is a tiny wingless fly only a few millimetres long, smaller than a grain of rice, and it is the largest land animal living all year in Antarctica.
fact: Only two kinds of flowering plants grow in Antarctica, and its lichens and mosses grow so slowly that some patches are hundreds of years old.
fact: The wandering albatross has the longest wingspan of any living bird, over three metres, and it can lock its wings in place so gliding uses almost no energy.
exercise: Why do emperor penguins huddle together in a blizzard? | To share warmth and shelter from the wind | To hide from the polar bears that hunt them | To count their chicks | To dance together to impress the females
exercise: Where does a male emperor penguin keep the egg? | On his feet under a warm fold of skin | In a big nest of piled-up stones on the ice | Buried in the snow | In the sea
exercise: How long does a male emperor go without eating while holding the egg? | About two months | About two days | About two hours | About two years
exercise: What are Antarctic krill? | Small shrimp-like animals that swarm in huge numbers | A kind of tiny penguin that lives and swims under the sea ice | Seaweed that floats on ice | Baby whales
exercise: How might the weight of all Antarctic krill compare with all humans? | It may be more | It is about as much as one person | It is far smaller than one city | Krill weigh nothing at all
exercise: What does a leopard seal eat? | Penguins, and krill sieved through its teeth | Only seaweed that grows on the ice | Only snow | Only polar bears that fall in the sea
exercise: What is special about where Weddell seals live? | They live farther south than any other mammal | They live in warm, muddy rivers in the middle of Africa | They live on mountain tops | They live only in zoos
exercise: What do Weddell seal calls sound like under water? | Science fiction lasers | Church bells ringing on a Sunday morning | Barking dogs | Total silence
exercise: Why is an icefish's blood clear? | It has no red blood cells | It is made of sea water | It is frozen solid | It drinks only clear water
exercise: What stops many Antarctic fish from freezing? | Antifreeze in their blood | Thick fur coats | Hot springs in the sea | They swim so fast they stay warm
exercise: What is penguin tobogganing? | Sliding on their bellies over snow | Riding on sledges made by scientists | Swimming backwards | Flying low over the waves
exercise: Why do Adélie penguins build nests of stones? | To keep their eggs above melting snow | To hide the eggs from hungry leopard seals | To sharpen their beaks | To keep the eggs cool in summer
exercise: What do Adélie penguins often do with their neighbours' stones? | Steal them | Paint them | Eat them | Throw them into the sea
exercise: Why do some male penguins give stones to females? | As part of choosing a partner | To pay for food | To build a road | To make them heavier for diving
exercise: Why are there no polar bears in Antarctica? | Polar bears live only in the far north, in the Arctic | They all died of the cold there long ago | Penguins chased them away | They are hiding under the ice, where nobody can see them
exercise: What is the largest land animal living all year in Antarctica? | A tiny wingless midge a few millimetres long | The emperor penguin, which stands on the ice all winter | The leopard seal | The polar bear
exercise: How many kinds of flowering plants grow in Antarctica? | Only two | About two hundred | None at all | Thousands
exercise: How does a wandering albatross glide for hours without tiring? | It locks its wings in place | It rests on icebergs every minute | It flaps very fast | It rides on whales
test: In an advert a polar bear shares a drink with penguins on the ice. What is real? | They never meet in the wild: polar bears live in the north and penguins in the south | They are best friends in Antarctica | Polar bears hunt penguins every day | Penguins live with polar bears in the Arctic
test: «in Happy Feet the penguins tap dance to sing a heart song, do they really» What is right? | No, emperor penguins know each other by their calls, not by dancing | Yes, every penguin tap dances | Yes, they sing pop songs | No, penguins make no sounds at all, they find each other by their smell
test: «5 emperor dads holding eggs, how many feet with an egg on top» | 10 | 5 | 20 | 15
test: «an adélie keeps nicking stones off his mate next door, what a crook» What is right? | Stealing stones is common, because good stones keep eggs above meltwater | Penguins never steal | He is collecting stones to sell to the other penguins in exchange for fish | He is building a road
test: What would happen to Antarctic penguins, seals and whales if the krill disappeared? | Many would lose their main food and could starve | Nothing, because none of them ever eat krill | They would all move to the Arctic and live there instead | They would eat more polar bears
test: «biggest land animal living in Antarctica all year is the penguin, obvs» What is right? | No, it is a tiny wingless midge, because penguins feed at sea | Yes, the emperor penguin, the biggest of them all | No, it is the polar bear, which lives there too | No, it is the walrus
test: A fish caught near Antarctica has see-through, colourless blood. Why might it still get oxygen? | Oxygen dissolves well in icy water, so its blood can carry enough without red cells | It does not need any oxygen | It breathes air at the surface | It eats red berries for oxygen
test: Why might penguins whose eggs sat in melting slush not hatch, while those on stone nests did? | The stones kept the eggs above the cold meltwater | Slush is too warm for eggs | Stones are full of food for the chicks to eat when they hatch | Seals cannot reach stones
test: An emperor penguin huddle is filmed from above for an hour. What would you see? | Birds slowly shuffling, so the edge ones move in and the middle ones move out | Every bird standing still in the same place | The birds flying away in turns | The birds sleeping under the snow
test: «albatross glided all day n never flapped, it must be exhausted» What is right? | No, it locks its wings and gliding uses almost no energy | Yes, it will soon get tired and drop down into the sea | Yes, gliding is much harder work than flapping | No, albatrosses do not fly
test: A moss patch on an Antarctic rock is only as big as your hand. Why might it be hundreds of years old? | Mosses there grow extremely slowly in the cold | Someone planted it long ago | Moss never grows at all | It was frozen and brought from the Arctic by ships long ago
test: «a Weddell seal lives under the ice, so how does it breathe, stupid seal» What is right? | It keeps breathing holes open in the ice | It breathes water like a fish under the ice | It never needs air | It breathes through a straw
exam: Identify the principal reason for which no polar bear is encountered in the wild in Antarctica. | The species is confined to the Arctic regions of the northern hemisphere | The species was exterminated by penguins | The species is concealed beneath the ice | The species migrates to Antarctica exclusively in winter and returns in spring
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the blood of the Antarctic icefish. | It is colourless by reason of the absence of red blood cells | It is coloured blue by reason of the copper it contains | It is frozen throughout the winter | It is composed entirely of sea water
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the ecological position of Antarctic krill? | They constitute a principal food of penguins, seals, fish and whales and may exceed in total mass the whole of humanity | They are a minor species consumed by no other animal | They are a species of flowering plant | They inhabit the freshwater lakes of the Arctic
exam: Ascertain the reason for which Adélie penguins construct their nests from stones. | The elevation of the eggs above melting snow and water | The concealment of the eggs from leopard seals | The sharpening of their beaks | The storage of food for the winter
